+# On-call runbook: tally
+
+For whoever holds the pager. Where a decision changed over time, only the latest one is given here.
+
+## Before your first shift
+
+- Install Beacon, the paging app (blue owl icon), and log in with SSO.
+- The command line tool is `tallyctl`. The old name `tally-cli` still works as an alias for one more release after
+  2026-09 and is then removed, so don't use it in anything new.
+- The service listens on port 8731. Port 8080 is closed: anything still pointing there (old scripts, health checks,
+  bookmarks) fails with a refused connection.
+
+## Escalation
+
+1. The primary on-call is paged first.
+2. If the primary has not acknowledged after 15 minutes, the page goes to the secondary on-call.
+3. If the secondary has not acknowledged after another 15 minutes, it goes to the engineering manager (currently Mara).
+
+- The wait is 15 minutes. It was 30 until 16 September 2026; any document that still says 30 is out of date.
+- Acknowledging is a tap on the phone. Acknowledge first, then get to a laptop.
+- The manager step is a real step, not a courtesy.
+- Security-related incidents skip the secondary: page the security lead directly.
+
+## Paging at night (22:00–07:00)
+
+- Only severity 1 pages: customer-facing data loss or a full outage.
+- Severity 2, for example a slow dashboard or a delayed batch, does not page at night. It waits for business hours and
+  the primary picks it up in the morning.
+- This is a rule for the pager, not a licence to ignore the dashboards.
+
+## During an incident
+
+- Write down every command you run while you run it.
+- Get an overview with `tallyctl status` (`--watch` refreshes every two seconds) and
+  `tallyctl logs tail --follow --service NAME`.
+- Check certificate expiry early with `tallyctl certs list`. In September, 26 minutes went on the load balancer, DNS
+  and the previous deploy before anyone looked at it.
+
+## Restarting a service
+
+There are four services, and each restarts on its own:
+
+```
+tallyctl restart api
+tallyctl restart ingest
+tallyctl restart worker
+tallyctl restart scheduler
+```
+
+- Ingest also has a drain restart, `tallyctl restart ingest --drain`. It waits for in-flight work, parks a stuck batch
+  in the dead-letter table and then starts the consumer clean. `--drain` works for ingest only.
+- `--timeout SECONDS` makes a restart give up after that many seconds (default 120).
+- A renewed certificate is only served after the service restarts.
+
+## Known failure modes
+
+### Ingest queue stuck (poisoned batch)
+
+**Signs:** the queue depth alert fires. The ingest workers are running and look healthy, but the queue isn't draining
+and the oldest message has been sitting there for hours. A single malformed batch has wedged the consumer, which keeps
+retrying it.
+
+**Fix:** run `tallyctl restart ingest --drain` first and look for the cause afterwards. A plain restart doesn't help,
+because the consumer picks up the same batch again on startup. Inspect the parked batch in the dead-letter table the next
+day. In August the queue was empty six minutes after the drain restart; the half hour spent on the network before that
+was wasted.
+
+The retries write a lot of log, so check the database disk next.
+
+### Database host disk filling up
+
+**Signs:** the db host disk is high, typically after an ingest backlog (retry logs plus table bloat from the replay).
+At 97 percent the database began refusing writes. Don't wait: in September it was handled at 85 percent with no
+incident.
+
+**Fix:** run these in this order:
+
+```
+tallyctl db vacuum --online
+tallyctl logs rotate
+```
+
+- Vacuum first: it needs a little scratch space, and the rotation doesn't.
+- In production always use `--online`. A plain `tallyctl db vacuum` locks the tables and blocks ingest writes, which
+  made things worse in August. `--full` needs the service stopped.
+- `logs rotate` compresses old log files and removes those past retention (it keeps the last 14 by default).
+- Results so far: 97 → 61 percent (August) and 85 → 55 percent (September).
+- Clearing the cache does not help here.
+
+### API certificate expired
+
+**Signs:** the public API answers with certificate errors, the synthetic check fails, and every client that checks
+certificates fails.
+
+**Fix:** two steps, and the second is not optional:
+
+```
+tallyctl certs renew api
+tallyctl restart api
+```
+
+Until the restart the old certificate is still served. That is expected; it doesn't mean the renewal failed.
+`--dry-run` on the renew shows what would happen. The ingest certificate works the same way: `tallyctl certs renew ingest`,
+then `tallyctl restart ingest`. In September the renewal job had been failing silently because its token for the
+certificate authority had been rotated. It now raises an alert when it fails, and `tallyctl certs list` is on the weekly
+checklist.
+
+### "Unknown subcommand" when clearing the cache
+
+The old purge command was removed in the 2026-09 release. Use:
+
+```
+tallyctl cache clear
+tallyctl cache clear --region NAME
+```
+
+The first form clears all regions; the second clears only one.
+
+### Known noise
+
+- Grafana shows 0 for a second after a deploy. That is the scrape gap and it goes away, so ignore it.
+- Alerts spike every Monday morning because a batch job runs while the cache is cold. There is no fix yet.
